            "raw_output": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "<cell id=\"d651fb69\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type># PyTorch Lightning baseline template\n\nby Andr\u00e9s Mu\u00f1oz-Jaramillo\n\nThis notebook is meant to act as a template to train and use a simple regression model to define a baseline that can be compared with a DS application.\n\nIt focuses on the concept of defining a PyTorch model, a PyTorch lightning training loop and the deffinition of metrics of performance.\n\nThis notebook assumes familiarity with the concepts of datasets and dataloaders contained in the **_0_dataset_dataloader_template.ipynb_**</cell id=\"d651fb69\">\n<cell id=\"f6bade9c\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Set your cuda visible device\n\n**IMPORTANT:** Since we are sharing resources, please make sure that the cuda visible device you put here is the one assigned to your team and your machine.   </cell id=\"f6bade9c\">\n<cell id=\"6d6af87a\">import os\nos.environ[\"CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\"] = \"0\"</cell id=\"6d6af87a\">\n<cell id=\"4ed04321\"># Must be set BEFORE torch is imported: cuBLAS reads this once, when it initializes, so\n# setting it later has no effect. It is what lets training.deterministic work without a\n# cuBLAS warning on every run. (Restart the kernel if torch was already imported.)\nimport os\nos.environ.setdefault(\"CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG\", \":4096:8\")\n\nimport sys\nfrom torch.utils.data import DataLoader\n\nimport torch\nimport yaml\n\nimport lightning as L\nfrom lightning.pytorch.callbacks import ModelCheckpoint\nfrom lightning.pytorch.loggers import CSVLogger, WandbLogger\n\n# Append base path.  May need to be modified if the folder structure changes.\n# It gives the notebook access to the wokshop_infrastructure folder.\nsys.path.append(\"../../\")\n \n# Append Surya path. May need to be modified if the folder structure changes.\n# It gives the notebook access to surya's release code.\n\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.utils import build_scalers  # Data scaling utilities for Surya stacks\n\ntorch.set_float32_matmul_precision('medium')\n\n</cell id=\"4ed04321\">\n<cell id=\"04135ff7\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Load configuration\n\nSurya was designed to read a configuration file that defines many aspects of the model\nincluding the data it uses we use this config file to set default values that do not\nneed to be modified, but also to define values specific to our downstream application</cell id=\"04135ff7\">\n<cell id=\"63c1fe60\"># The config is the single source of truth. load_flare_config() parses it into a typed\n# object, exactly as the training script 3_finetune_template_1D.py does, so the same YAML\n# behaves identically here and in production. Notebook 0 walks through what it contains.\nfrom downstream_apps.solar_event.configs import solar_event_config\n\ncfg = solar_event_config(\"./configs/config_script.yaml\")\nprint(f\"Loaded config for job: {cfg.job_id}\")\n</cell id=\"63c1fe60\">\n\nLoaded config for job: solar_event_identification\n\n<cell id=\"70dfbfb8\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Download assets\n\nThe config says where the assets belong, so it is loaded first. `ensure_assets()` fetches only what is missing from HuggingFace, so re-running this is free.\n</cell id=\"70dfbfb8\">\n<cell id=\"b23545ce\"># One implementation, shared by the notebooks, the training script and the\n# download_*.sh wrappers: workshop_infrastructure/assets.py.\n# The linear baseline needs no backbone, so skip the 1.8 GB weights.\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.assets import ensure_assets\n\nensure_assets(cfg, which=[\"scalers\"])\n\n# Now that scalers.yaml is guaranteed to be on disk, load it. build_scalers()\n# accepts the resolved path directly.\nscalers = build_scalers(info=cfg.data.scalers_path)\nprint(f\"Loaded scalers for {len(scalers)} channels.\")\n</cell id=\"b23545ce\">\n\nLoaded scalers for 13 channels.\n\n\n\n<cell id=\"af6574dc\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Define Downstream (DS) datasets\n\nThis child class takes as input all expected HelioFM parameters, plus additonal parameters relevant to the downstream application.  Here we focus in particular to the DS index and parameters necessary to combine it with the HelioFM index.\n\nAnother important component of creating a dataset class for your DS is normalization.  Here we use a log normalization on xray flux that will act as the output target.  Making log10(xray_flux) strictly positive and having 66% of its values between 0 and 1\n\nIn this case we will define both a training and a validation dataset using the indices pointed at in the config\n\n**_Important:  In this notebook we sets max_number_of_samples=6 to potentially avoid going through the whole dataset as we explore it.  Keep in mind this for the future in case the database seems smaller than you expect_**\n</cell id=\"af6574dc\">\n<cell id=\"34db2cc6\">from downstream_apps.solar_event.datasets.solar_event_dataset import solar_Dataset</cell id=\"34db2cc6\">\n<cell id=\"70e9c5b2\"># build_helio_dataloaders() constructs the train and validation datasets and wraps them\n# in DataLoaders. It fills in every generic argument (channels, temporal sampling, S3\n# access, worker settings) from the config \u2014 see notebook 0 for what that block looks\n# like written out. Only the flare-specific arguments are passed here, which is exactly\n# the list you replace when you fork the template.\n#\n# It also handles two details that are easy to get wrong by hand: the validation set gets\n# phase=\"val\" (no random channel masking or flips), and only the training loader shuffles.\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.datasets.builders import build_helio_dataloaders\n\ntrain_data_loader, val_data_loader = build_helio_dataloaders(\n    cfg,\n    solar_Dataset,\n    scalers=scalers,\n    num_workers=4,          # fewer workers than the script: notebooks start faster\n    #### Downstream (DS) specific parameters\n    return_surya_stack=True,\n    max_number_of_samples=6,\n    ds_flare_index_path=f\"data/{cfg.data.solar_event_path}\",\n    ds_time_column=cfg.data.ds_time_column,\n    ds_time_tolerance=cfg.data.ds_time_tolerance,\n    ds_match_direction=cfg.data.ds_match_direction,\n)\n\nbatch_size = cfg.batch_size\nprint(f\"train: {len(train_data_loader.dataset)} samples | \"\n      f\"val: {len(val_data_loader.dataset)} samples | batch_size: {batch_size}\")\n</cell id=\"70e9c5b2\">\n\ntrain: 6 samples | val: 6 samples | batch_size: 2\n\n<cell id=\"79fda7cf\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>Training and validation get separate datasets and dataloaders. They differ only in the index they read and in `phase`: `phase=\"val\"` turns off the random channel masking and vertical flips used for training augmentation.\n\nThe loaders use `multiprocessing_context=\"spawn\"` \u2014 the dataset holds an S3 client that does not survive `fork`, and spawn also avoids lockups in shared environments.\n</cell id=\"79fda7cf\">\n<cell id=\"48bb2912\">'''# Inspect a single batch to confirm shapes before training.\nbatch = next(iter(train_data_loader))\nprint({k: (tuple(v.shape) if hasattr(v, \"shape\") else type(v).__name__) for k, v in batch.items()})\n''' \n\n# This code seems to crash cpu server</cell id=\"48bb2912\">\n\n/home/jovyan/envs/surya_ws/lib/python3.12/site-packages/torch/utils/data/dataloader.py:668: UserWarning: 'pin_memory' argument is set as true but no accelerator is found, then device pinned memory won't be used.\n  warnings.warn(warn_msg)\n\n<cell id=\"1d4d2dea\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Define simple baseline model\n\nDefining a simple baseline is important to understand what value is bringing the AI model to the problem.  \n\nIt is always very good to have a very simple baseline model.  Ideally one that cannot overfit the data.  This is a very good way of really measuring the value added of complex models.   Classical machine learning excels here:\n\n- Regressions and logistic regressions.\n- Climatological averages.\n- Persistance.\n- Simple transformations.\n\nSimple models avoid excesively optimistic assessments of the capatiblities of a complex models and for many problems are actually remarkably hard to beat.\n\nIn this example we define a simple regression acting on the intensity of each channel.  Note that we invert the normalization to deal with strictly positive quantities.  As with the dataset we will be importing the model from a module so that we can use it within training scripts later on.</cell id=\"1d4d2dea\">\n<cell id=\"3f5fd639\">from downstream_apps.template.models.simple_baseline import RegressionFlareModel</cell id=\"3f5fd639\">\n<cell id=\"18d87992\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>We can now test that this model manipulates a batch as expected and returns an estimate of flare intensity\n\nNote that the simple regression model definition requires knowing the number of channels and timesteps so here we pull that information from the configuration intializing the model.</cell id=\"18d87992\">\n<cell id=\"b0b6ce02\">n_input_timestamps = cfg.model.time_embedding.time_dim\nn_channels = len(cfg.data.channels)\n\n# RegressionFlareModel only needs the flattened input dimension.\n# It expects 'ts' in signum-log space \u2014 use destandardize_channels() before calling forward().\nmodel = RegressionFlareModel(n_input_timestamps * n_channels)\n</cell id=\"b0b6ce02\">\n<cell id=\"3fee1e68\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>Now we can pass the input stack 'ts' to the model to transform it into our regression output.   Note that since this model has not been trained and was initalized randomly.  The output here has no real meaning.  It only acts as a test that our model forward doesn't have dimension problems.\n\nDimension problemns are the dominant source of error in this kind of work.\n\nNote that our output has now the size of our batch.</cell id=\"3fee1e68\">\n<cell id=\"d7d97841\">from downstream_apps.template.models.simple_baseline import destandardize_channels\n\nbatch = next(iter(train_data_loader))\n\n# RegressionFlareModel works in signum-log space, not normalized space.\n# destandardize_channels undoes the per-channel z-score but KEEPS the log compression \u2014\n# raw DN values span too many orders of magnitude to be good features for one linear layer.\n# (For true physical units, use train_dataset.inverse_transform_data() instead. See the\n#  \"THE THREE SPACES\" block in workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py.)\nbatch_logspace = destandardize_channels(batch, channel_order=cfg.data.channels, scalers=scalers)\noutput = model.forward(batch_logspace)[:, 0]  # Get rid of singleton dimension\noutput\n</cell id=\"d7d97841\">\n<cell id=\"36f32e64\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Define your metrics\n\nMetrics are a very important part of training AI models.   They provide your models with the quantitification of error, which in turn shifts the weights towards better pefrorming models.  They also provide a way for you to monitor performance, identify overfitting, and quantify value added. \n\nWe now initialize the metrics class which allows you to control what metrics do you want to use as \"loss\" (i.e. the metrics that backpropagate through your model) and which ones for monitoring performance.  As with other components, this takes the form of a loaded module that can be later use in a training script</cell id=\"36f32e64\">\n<cell id=\"34c502ec\">from downstream_apps.template.metrics.template_metrics import FlareMetrics</cell id=\"34c502ec\">\n<cell id=\"d152b127\">train_loss_metrics = FlareMetrics(\"train_loss\")\n# val_loss is the quantity logged as \"val_loss\" and used to pick the best checkpoint.\n# It defaults to the same MSE as train_loss \u2014 override FlareMetrics.val_loss to change it.\nval_loss_metrics = FlareMetrics(\"val_loss\")\ntrain_evaluation_metrics = FlareMetrics(\"train_metrics\")\n# Reported only: val_metrics do NOT influence checkpoint selection.\nvalidation_evaluation_metrics = FlareMetrics(\"val_metrics\")</cell id=\"d152b127\">\n<cell id=\"0550062c\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>Now they can be evaluated in our model's output and our ground truth.   First the loss that actually will backpropagate, in this case Mean Squared Errror</cell id=\"0550062c\">\n<cell id=\"2baf9d36\">train_loss_metrics(output, batch[\"forecast\"])</cell id=\"2baf9d36\">\n<cell id=\"3dc5eb6d\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>Then a training evaluation that will not backpropagate and inform our model, but that we can keep an eye on. Note that reporting lots of metrics during training will slow the training process.  I'm including it her as an example, but oftentimes is better to put the diagnostics only in the validation evaluation metrics.\n\nHere we are caclulating the Root Relative Squared Error https://lightning.ai/docs/torchmetrics/stable/regression/rse.html \n\nA value below one means the prediction is better than predicting the average.  It is unlikely that this metric will be lower than one with a randomly initialized model</cell id=\"3dc5eb6d\">\n<cell id=\"796559f1\">train_evaluation_metrics(output, batch[\"forecast\"])</cell id=\"796559f1\">\n<cell id=\"3d65e7cd\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>In the validation evaluation metrics we report both MSE and RRSE</cell id=\"3d65e7cd\">\n<cell id=\"0a0bbb35\">validation_evaluation_metrics(output, batch[\"forecast\"])</cell id=\"0a0bbb35\">\n<cell id=\"537871ed\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Define your PyTorch ligthning module\n\nIn this workshop we will use PyTorch lightning to train our models.  PyTorch lighting reduces the amount of code required to implement a training loop in comparison to PyTorch (at the expense of control and versatility).  \n\nOpening the FlareLightningModule shows a simple Lightning model implementation.  It consists of:\n\n- An initialization of the class (metrics, model, and learning rate).\n- The forward code that runs evaluation of the model.\n- Training and validation steps.\n- Configuration of optimizers.</cell id=\"537871ed\">\n<cell id=\"9d730cf3\">from downstream_apps.template.lightning_modules.pl_simple_baseline import FlareLightningModule</cell id=\"9d730cf3\">\n<cell id=\"3321963d\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Set your global seeds\n\nSince training AI models generally uses stochastic gradient descent, it is a good idea to fix your random seeds so that your training exercise is reproducible.    </cell id=\"3321963d\">\n<cell id=\"cc28903d\">L.seed_everything(42, workers=True)</cell id=\"cc28903d\">\n<cell id=\"119ec413\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Intialize Lightning module\n\nNow we properly initalize the Lightning module to enable training, including passing the dictionary of metrics</cell id=\"119ec413\">\n<cell id=\"553565e7\">from functools import partial\n\nmetrics = {\n    'train_loss': train_loss_metrics,\n    'val_loss': val_loss_metrics,\n    'train_metrics': train_evaluation_metrics,\n    'val_metrics': validation_evaluation_metrics,\n}\n\n# Wire up the inverse transform so FlareLightningModule applies it before every model call.\npreprocess_fn = partial(\n    destandardize_channels,\n    channel_order=cfg.data.channels,\n    scalers=scalers,\n)\n\nlit_model = FlareLightningModule(\n    model, metrics, lr=cfg.learning_rate, batch_size=batch_size, preprocess_fn=preprocess_fn\n)\n</cell id=\"553565e7\">\n<cell id=\"7b9c76c8\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Logging\n\nIn order to properly compare experiments against each other, it is very useful to log evaluation metrics in a place where they can be compared against other training runs.  In this workshop we will use Weights and Biases (WandB). \n\nThe first time you run WandB in a machine it will ask you to login to WandB.  You should have received an invitation to our project.  In order to login you must:\n\n- Select option 2 (existing account).   In VScode the dialog opens a box at the top of your screen.\n- Click on get API Key (this will open a browser).\n- Generate API Key.\n- Paste it in the dialog box at the top of your VSCode</cell id=\"7b9c76c8\">\n<cell id=\"48c4d138\">project_name = cfg.wandb_project\nrun_name = \"baseline_experiment_1\"  # give your run a descriptive name\n\nwandb_logger = WandbLogger(\n    entity=cfg.wandb_entity,  # set wandb_entity in the config; null = personal account\n    project=project_name,\n    name=run_name,\n    log_model=False,\n    save_dir=\"./wandb/wandb_tmp\",\n)\n\ncsv_logger = CSVLogger(\"runs\", name=project_name)\n</cell id=\"48c4d138\">\n<cell id=\"7e24e547\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Initialize trainer\n\nWith the loggers done, now the trainer needs to be defined.  The trainer defines several properties of your training run. Here we define:\n\n- The max number of epochs (one epoch represents your model seeing your entire training dataset).\n- Define where the training run will take place (auto uses the GPU if possible, if not, CPU).\n- The loggers.\n- The callbacks (here we save the model with the lowest validation loss).\n- Logging frequency (because we are working with a small dataset it needs to be small).</cell id=\"7e24e547\">\n<cell id=\"b008c604\">max_epochs = 2\n\n# -------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Trainer\n# -------------------------------------------------------------------------\ntrainer = L.Trainer(\n    max_epochs=max_epochs,\n    accelerator=\"auto\",\n    devices=\"auto\",\n    logger=[wandb_logger, csv_logger],\n    callbacks=[\n        ModelCheckpoint(\n            monitor=\"val_loss\",\n            mode=\"min\",\n            save_top_k=1,\n        )\n    ],\n    log_every_n_steps=2,\n)</cell id=\"b008c604\">\n<cell id=\"1088d108\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Fit the model\n\nFinally we fit the model.  We pass the Lighting module, and our dataloaders. (This cell requires the use of GPU, every other cell can be run on CPU.)</cell id=\"1088d108\">\n<cell id=\"f8a6e448\">trainer.fit(lit_model, train_data_loader, val_data_loader)</cell id=\"f8a6e448\">\n<cell id=\"ebcc1a23\"><cell_type>markdown</cell_type>## Conclusion\n\nWith this we have now integrated our dataset, dataloaders, metrics, and baseline into an end-2-end training loop.  The next step is to substitute the simple model with Surya.</cell id=\"ebcc1a23\">"
              }
            ],
            "tool_call_id": "toolu_bdrk_01MPZC8i7JGDzQogzwR9Sm8X",
            "title": "Read 1_baseline_template.ipynb",
            "raw_input": {
              "file_path": "/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/solar_event/1_baseline_template.ipynb"
            },
            "kind": "read"
          },
          {
            "selected_option_id": "allow-once",
            "raw_output": "1\t\"\"\"\n2\tDataset and DataLoader construction shared by every Surya downstream app.\n3\t\n4\t``HelioNetCDFDataset`` takes ~20 constructor arguments, all of which come straight from\n5\tthe ``data:`` and ``training:`` sections of the config. Spelling that block out at every\n6\tentry point means the same 20 lines get copied into every notebook and every training\n7\tscript, and they drift: one copy forgets ``phase=\"val\"``, another hardcodes a flag the\n8\tYAML was supposed to control.\n9\t\n10\tThese builders own that block once. A downstream app supplies its dataset subclass and\n11\twhatever task-specific keyword arguments that subclass adds::\n12\t\n13\t    train_loader, val_loader = build_helio_dataloaders(\n14\t        cfg,\n15\t        FlareDSDataset,\n16\t        ds_flare_index_path=cfg.data.flare_index_path,\n17\t        label_transform=my_label_transform,\n18\t    )\n19\t\"\"\"\n20\t\n21\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n22\t\n23\timport random\n24\tfrom functools import partial\n25\tfrom typing import Any, Tuple, Type\n26\t\n27\timport numpy as np\n28\timport torch\n29\tfrom torch.utils.data import DataLoader, Dataset\n30\t\n31\tfrom workshop_infrastructure.utils import build_scalers\n32\t\n33\t\n34\tdef _seed_worker(worker_id: int, base_seed: int) -> None:\n35\t    \"\"\"Seed every RNG a DataLoader worker might draw from.\n36\t\n37\t    Seeding torch alone is not enough: ``RandomChannelMaskerTransform`` in\n38\t    ``workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py`` draws from Python's ``random``\n39\t    (``random.sample`` / ``random.random``), and downstream datasets commonly use NumPy.\n40\t\n41\t    Supplying this deliberately displaces Lightning's auto-injected\n42\t    ``pl_worker_init_function``, which derives worker seeds from ``torch.initial_seed()``\n43\t    \u2014 i.e. from the ambient global RNG state, which is exactly the coupling we are\n44\t    removing here.\n45\t    \"\"\"\n46\t    seed = (base_seed + worker_id) % (2 ** 32)\n47\t    random.seed(seed)\n48\t    np.random.seed(seed)\n49\t    torch.manual_seed(seed)\n50\t\n51\t\n52\tdef _base_dataset_kwargs(cfg, scalers) -> dict:\n53\t    \"\"\"Map a TrainingConfig onto the HelioNetCDFDataset constructor arguments.\n54\t\n55\t    This is the single place where config field names meet dataset parameter names.\n56\t    \"\"\"\n57\t    return dict(\n58\t        # Temporal sampling\n59\t        time_delta_input_minutes=cfg.data.time_delta_input_minutes,\n60\t        time_delta_target_minutes=cfg.data.time_delta_target_minutes,\n61\t        n_input_timestamps=cfg.model.time_embedding.time_dim,\n62\t        rollout_steps=cfg.rollout_steps,\n63\t        # Channels and normalization\n64\t        channels=cfg.data.channels,\n65\t        scalers=scalers,\n66\t        # Augmentation\n67\t        drop_hmi_probability=cfg.drop_hmi_probability,\n68\t        use_latitude_in_learned_flow=cfg.use_latitude_in_learned_flow,\n69\t        # Storage: local root, and how s3:// paths in the index are read\n70\t        sdo_data_root_path=cfg.data.sdo_data_root_path,\n71\t        s3_mode=cfg.data.s3_mode,\n72\t        s3_storage_options={\"anon\": cfg.data.s3_anon},\n73\t        s3_cache_dir=cfg.data.s3_cache_dir,\n74\t        s3_boto3_max_concurrency=cfg.data.s3_boto3_max_concurrency,\n75\t        s3_boto3_part_size_mb=cfg.data.s3_boto3_part_size_mb,\n76\t    )\n77\t\n78\t\n79\tdef build_helio_datasets(\n80\t    cfg,\n81\t    dataset_cls: Type[Dataset],\n82\t    scalers: Any = None,\n83\t    **task_kwargs,\n84\t) -> Tuple[Dataset, Dataset]:\n85\t    \"\"\"Build the train and validation datasets described by ``cfg``.\n86\t\n87\t    Args:\n88\t        cfg: A ``TrainingConfig`` from ``load_config()``.\n89\t        dataset_cls: ``HelioNetCDFDataset`` or a subclass of it.\n90\t        scalers: Normalization statistics. Built from ``cfg.data.scalers_path`` if omitted;\n91\t            pass an existing dict to avoid re-reading the YAML.\n92\t        **task_kwargs: Extra keyword arguments forwarded to ``dataset_cls`` \u2014 the\n93\t            task-specific parameters your subclass adds.\n94\t\n95\t    Returns:\n96\t        ``(train_dataset, val_dataset)``. They differ only in the index they read and in\n97\t        ``phase``: the validation set uses ``phase=\"val\"``, which disables the random\n98\t        channel masking and vertical flips that are applied during training.\n99\t    \"\"\"\n100\t    if scalers is None:\n101\t        scalers = build_scalers(info=cfg.data.scalers_path)\n102\t\n103\t    common = {**_base_dataset_kwargs(cfg, scalers), **task_kwargs}\n104\t\n105\t    train_dataset = dataset_cls(index_path=cfg.data.train_data_path, phase=\"train\", **common)\n106\t    val_dataset = dataset_cls(index_path=cfg.data.valid_data_path, phase=\"val\", **common)\n107\t    return train_dataset, val_dataset\n108\t\n109\t\n110\tdef build_helio_dataloaders(\n111\t    cfg,\n112\t    dataset_cls: Type[Dataset],\n113\t    scalers: Any = None,\n114\t    num_workers: int | None = None,\n115\t    seed: int | None = None,\n116\t    **task_kwargs,\n117\t) -> Tuple[DataLoader, DataLoader]:\n118\t    \"\"\"Build the train and validation DataLoaders described by ``cfg``.\n119\t\n120\t    Args:\n121\t        cfg: A ``TrainingConfig`` from ``load_config()``.\n122\t        dataset_cls: ``HelioNetCDFDataset`` or a subclass of it.\n123\t        scalers: Normalization statistics; built from the config if omitted.\n124\t        num_workers: Override for ``cfg.num_workers`` (useful in notebooks, where fewer\n125\t            workers start faster).\n126\t        seed: Seed for the shuffle order and the per-worker RNGs. Defaults to ``cfg.seed``.\n127\t            Pinning it explicitly is what makes the epoch order depend only on the config:\n128\t            a bare ``shuffle=True`` seeds its sampler from whatever the global torch RNG\n129\t            state happens to be when the iterator is created, so anything that consumes\n130\t            RNG earlier in the program silently reshuffles the data.\n131\t        **task_kwargs: Extra keyword arguments forwarded to ``dataset_cls``.\n132\t\n133\t    Returns:\n134\t        ``(train_loader, val_loader)``. Only the training loader shuffles.\n135\t    \"\"\"\n136\t    train_dataset, val_dataset = build_helio_datasets(\n137\t        cfg, dataset_cls, scalers=scalers, **task_kwargs\n138\t    )\n139\t\n140\t    workers = cfg.num_workers if num_workers is None else num_workers\n141\t    base_seed = cfg.seed if seed is None else seed\n142\t\n143\t    loader_kwargs = dict(\n144\t        batch_size=cfg.batch_size,\n145\t        num_workers=workers,\n146\t        pin_memory=True,\n147\t        drop_last=True,\n148\t    )\n149\t    if workers > 0:\n150\t        # \"spawn\": the dataset holds an s3fs/boto3 handle that does not survive fork.\n151\t        # Both of these are rejected outright when num_workers == 0.\n152\t        loader_kwargs[\"multiprocessing_context\"] = \"spawn\"\n153\t        loader_kwargs[\"persistent_workers\"] = True\n154\t        loader_kwargs[\"worker_init_fn\"] = partial(_seed_worker, base_seed=base_seed)\n155\t\n156\t    # An explicit generator makes the shuffle a function of the seed alone, rather than\n157\t    # of the global RNG state at the moment the iterator happens to be created.\n158\t    shuffle_generator = torch.Generator()\n159\t    shuffle_generator.manual_seed(base_seed)\n160\t\n161\t    train_loader = DataLoader(\n162\t        train_dataset, shuffle=True, generator=shuffle_generator, **loader_kwargs\n163\t    )\n164\t    # No generator for validation: it is not shuffled, so there is nothing to seed.\n165\t    val_loader = DataLoader(val_dataset, shuffle=False, **loader_kwargs)\n166\t    return train_loader, val_loader\n167\t",
            "raw_input": {
              "file_path": "/home/jovyan/surya_workshop/workshop_infrastructure/datasets/builders.py"
            },
            "tool_call_id": "toolu_bdrk_013xcpjAHoiq8GV6zn6rhhjE",
            "title": "Read builders.py",
            "session_id": "66397102-bff9-4b08-8066-fcf62070788a",
