      "type": "msg",
      "id": "e676932b-54d9-4d7b-912e-005c89645e70",
      "body": "Found it. The crash is memory exhaustion, not a bug in the cell's code \u2014 the logs and cache directory confirm it.\n\n**Evidence:**\n- Every log in `logs/data/*.log` ends mid-download, e.g. `Reading file s3://nasa-surya-bench/2010/05/20100518_0000.nc.` with no exception, no traceback, nothing after. That pattern \u2014 a worker just stops logging \u2014 is the signature of a process getting `SIGKILL`ed by the OOM killer, not a Python error.\n- The S3 cache (`/home/jovyan/scratch_space/midhun/surya_cache`) holds ~585 MB compressed `.nc` files, several downloaded in the same run (`20260915T182532Z`), matching multiple workers pulling data concurrently.\n- `free -h` shows only ~5.3 GiB free / ~16 GiB available right now, on a 30 GiB box that's shared with other users (the notebook itself warns \"we are sharing resources\").\n\n**Why it blows up memory:** `build_helio_dataloaders(..., num_workers=4)` sets `multiprocessing_context=\"spawn\"`, `persistent_workers=True`, and PyTorch's default `prefetch_factor=2`. So up to `4 workers \u00d7 2 prefetch = 8` full-resolution samples can be in flight simultaneously. Each sample is a 13-channel, 4096\u00d74096, float32 stack \u2014 `13*4096*4096*4 bytes \u2248 832 MiB` for the raw tensor alone, before the intermediate copies from decompressing the NetCDF and the signum-log/z-score normalization. That's easily 1.5\u20132+ GiB of transient memory per sample once you count intermediates. 8 of those in flight at once is well over 10 GiB just for this dataloader \u2014 on top of the S3 download itself, which uses `s3_boto3_max_concurrency: 4` threads \u00d7 `part_size_mb: 64` per worker, so 4 workers \u00d7 4 threads = 16 concurrent 64 MB download buffers too.\n\nOn a shared, memory-constrained JupyterHub container, that's enough to push the kernel (or a sibling process on the node) over its memory limit, and the OOM killer takes down the kernel \u2014 which looks like \"the server crashed\" with no Python-level error to explain it.\n\nThe `'''...'''` wrapping and the trailing comment (`# This code seems to crash cpu server`) confirm someone already hit this and disabled the cell as a workaround rather than a fix \u2014 it currently doesn't execute anything (it's just a string literal), so the pin_memory warning shown below it is stale output from an earlier run before the string wrap was added.\n\n**Fix, not workaround:** drop concurrency for this sanity check. In the notebook, either:\n- Call `build_helio_dataloaders(..., num_workers=0)` for this inspection (single-process, no spawn, no prefetch pile-up), or\n- Keep `num_workers=4` but lower `prefetch_factor` and check `max_number_of_samples=6` is actually being honored before the full loader spins up all workers.\n\nWant me to unwrap the cell and switch it to `num_workers=0` (or otherwise reduce concurrency) so it actually runs the shape check safely?",
      "raw_time": false,
      "time": 1789498097.0352407,
      "mentions": [],
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona"
    },
    {
      "metadata": {
        "to_persona": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
        "model": {
          "id": "sonnet",
          "settings": {}
