      "time": 1789572164.366622,
      "mentions": [],
      "body": "Reading the shapes off `helio.py`'s `__getitem__` docstring (`ts: (C, T, H, W)` per sample) plus what `solar_Dataset` overrides, each key means:\n\n- **`ts`: `(2, 13, 1, 4096, 4096)`** \u2014 `(B, C, T, H, W)`. Batch of 2 (`cfg.batch_size`), 13 channels (8 AIA wavelengths + 5 HMI components), `T=1` input timestamp (`cfg.model.time_embedding.time_dim`), each a full-resolution 4096\u00d74096 solar image. This is the Surya input stack.\n\n- **`time_delta_input`: `(2, 1)`** \u2014 `(B, T)`. For each of the 2 samples, the offset (in hours, relative to the \"now\" frame) of each of the `T=1` input frames. Since there's only one input timestep and it *is* \"now\", every value here is `0.0`.\n\n- **`forecast`: `(2,)`** \u2014 one scalar per sample. This is *not* image frames \u2014 `solar_Dataset.__getitem__` (in `solar_event_dataset.py:124`) overwrites the base class's `forecast` key with the flare label (`Solar_Event_Occurrence`, cast to `float32`) matched from the flare catalog. `load_forecast_frames=False` is set by the dataset specifically so future Surya frames are never fetched, since this task supplies its own label instead of predicting future imagery.\n\n- **`ds_index`: `'list'`** \u2014 not a tensor, so PyTorch's default `collate_fn` left it as a plain Python list of length 2 (one string per sample). Each entry is the ISO-format timestamp from the flare catalog that this sample was matched to (`solar_event_dataset.py:125`) \u2014 useful for tracing a batch element back to a specific catalog row.\n\nSo the batch is well-formed: 2 samples, each a single 13-channel full-disk image plus a binary/scalar flare-occurrence target and its matched catalog timestamp \u2014 exactly what the regression baseline (`RegressionFlareModel`) expects as input/target."
    },
